      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we need to use the standard form of the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin. The standard form is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a\\) is the semimajor axis and \\(b\\) is the semiminor axis. From the given ellipse, we can see that the foci are located at \\((\\pm c, 0)\\), where \\(c\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the diagram, we can identify that the lengths of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are 6 and 2 respectively. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\na = 6 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we can calculate \\(c\\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{36 - 4} = \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci is the distance between \\((4\\sqrt{2}, 0)\\) and \\((-4\\sqrt{2}, 0)\\), which is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2 \\times 4\\sqrt{2} = 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]",
